name 'csp-unit2-lesson8-cfu1_27_28'
title 'Check Your Understanding'
description ''
question 'A streaming service lets you watch a show if you have a premium account, or it\’s within the show\’s free-preview window. Given `has_premium` and `in_preview_window`, which expression correctly decides whether someone can watch?'
wrong '`has_premium and in_preview_window`'
right '`has_premium or in_preview_window`'
wrong '`not has_premium and in_preview_window`'
wrong '`has_premium or not in_preview_window`'
allow_multiple_attempts false

teacher_markdown <<MARKDOWN
Teacher solution and distractor rationale:

- `has_premium and in_preview_window`: Requires both conditions at once, which would incorrectly deny a premium user who’s outside the preview window — too restrictive for an “or” decision.
- `has_premium or in_preview_window`: (correct): The scenario’s own wording — “or” — means either condition alone is enough.
- `not has_premium and in_preview_window`: Backwards — this only grants access to non-premium users during the preview window, and denies every premium user outright.
- `has_premium or not in_preview_window`: Grants access in almost every case (true unless someone is premium and the window is closed) — overly permissive, and doesn’t match the described decision at all.
MARKDOWN
